A||||22||kornholio||spark plug questions
Z||000000||kornholio||08-28-99||10:15 AM||||I have a 2.8 Auto SE.<br>1. What benefits will I get if I replace my spark plugs?<br>2. What kind of plugs should I get (brand, V-tip)?<br>3. Is there a way to torque them without using a torque wrench?<br>4. Is it hard to get at the ones on the firewall side?<p>Thanks.
Z||000001||theogre||08-28-99||11:04 AM||||1&2. You may bet better starting depending on just what the condition of the current plugs is and what you put in. I've used the regular split fires w/o problem, But others hate them. Accel Ugrove has a good rep. Performance wise, no plug is signifigantly better than another at boosting power.<p>3. You have to check the specific plugs you get. The old rules of 1/8 turn after contact for taper, and 1/4 turn after contact for gaskets, don't always apply to new plugs. If you use Anti Seize compound use EXTREAM CARE or you WILL over torq the plugs. The anti sieze lubricates the thread. Spark plug torq specs are for DRY threads. Exceeding the reomended torq can damage, even ruin, the plugs easily.<p>4. Yeah, it's a pain but it can be done. take out the dog bone and rock back the motor to get better access.
Z||000002||Dale||08-28-99||12:43 PM||||Check out the new Bosch platinum plugs, I have them in mine and like them.  If you are lokking for a little bit more spark etc. replace the wires with a good set.  I run 8.8mm, they are not cheap but again I do like them.
Z||000003||theogre||08-28-99||08:50 PM||||Don't use any of the platinum plugs on the DIS motor..... The tiny platinum electrod doesn't seem to like the waste spark. I've gotten multiple reports of problems.<p>I use good wires.... Usually MSW's from pep boys of all places. Even on the high power DIS motor they are running just fine. They are not grosly expencive, have lifetime waranty(which I did have ocasion to test once, 3 years after I bought the set. A boot leaked water and a terminal rusted out.<p>Currently my DIS motor has the Split fires, and they have 20K on them. Still working fine. Shop around if you buy spltifire. The list for them is about 3$ each now.
Z||000004||Bruce||08-29-99||11:47 AM||||Ogre,<br>What is the DIS motor?
Z||000005||batboy||08-29-99||12:05 PM||||You can't go wrong with Accel plugs and wires. DIS means Distributorless Ignition System. These later model DIS engines use "coil packs" and a crank sensor instead of a distributor.
Z||000006||theogre||08-29-99||01:36 PM||||Actually, while it would make sense DIS means distributorless, GM actually defines it as Direct Ignition....... Go figure...<p>The DIS system uses 2 coils, on a 4 cyl, firing plugs in pairs. The second plug in each pair is on a cyl in exhaust when it fires. It's a very reliable system most of the time. After all, there are no moving parts in it. When it screws up tho, it can be a real bitch. Especially if the crank sensor needs changing. The DIS brick is down under the intake manifold which makes it a mother to get to in Fiero.
Z||000007||Dale||08-29-99||02:50 PM||||Good call on the DIS Ogre,  I didn't even think of it becouse mine is a standerd ignition.  The wires I have are Accell.
Z||000008||4Banger Bandit||08-29-99||03:51 PM||||I just changed the plugs on my 87 GT to Delco rapid Fires and I never had any trouble with the rear plugs.  Some people recommend to remove the decklid, but it really shouldn't be necessary.  Just feel your way around.  Good luck!
Z||000009||Bruce||08-29-99||04:14 PM||||I take it, then, that the DIS system is an after market addition? I'm still learning the nuances of my V6.
Z||000010||batboy||08-29-99||07:00 PM||||Bruce, no it's a factory change over thing. The 2.5 began using the DIS in 1987 or so. I'm not sure about the 2.8, maybe 1988? All of the 3.1 engines I've seen are DIS motors.
Z||000011||Bruce||08-29-99||08:30 PM||||Thanks! I was a little hesitant to install the Bosch platnum plugs after reading what Ogre said about potential problems with the DIS motor. My '86GT isn't so equipped.<br>However, with the guidance of Forum contribution, I went ahead and installed the plugs. In between groping and cursing, i finally got them all in. I'm now going to soak my hands for the remainder of the evening! 
Z||000012||theogre||08-29-99||08:33 PM||||I pretty much always include the warning about DIS motors. Even tho this thread started about a 2.8, 4 cyl readers will use the data. Only the 4cyl was available with DIS starting in 87 for Fiero. DIS has also been used in 6 and 8 cyl since.
Z||000013||Cooter||08-29-99||09:46 PM||||Does the 88 v6 use the DIS system??
Z||000014||batboy||08-30-99||05:25 AM||||Ogre says no, only 1987 and newer 2.5 engines on Fieros use the DIS. I was probably wrong when I said 1988 on the 2.8 liters. Seems like they went to the serpentine belt at the same time they went to the DIS too, and I don't believe any of the Fiero 2.8 engines had the serpentine belts.
Z||000015||PaUL||08-31-99||06:13 PM||||Ive been thinking about getting new sparkplug wires, either Accel 300+ or MSD SuperConductor. Ive read that both have much lower resistance per foot of wire than carbon core wires but with the same RF protection, although I think the MSD wires' name is sort of false advertising. Its not actually a superconductor. Anyone here have any experience with either of these wires? 
Z||000016||Dale||08-31-99||11:38 PM||||THe 8.8 wires that I run are the Accel 300+, they are great wires.  Only bummer was they didn't produce any for the 4 or 6 cyl motors at the time ( about a year ago ) so I have 4 spare wires.  Oh well!!!!
Z||000017||Dale||08-31-99||11:41 PM||||forgot to mention Summit has them for $58.<p>Aloha
Z||000018||ChadMan||09-01-99||07:28 AM||||My 88 Coupe with the DIS 2.5 runs just great with ACDelco factory-type spark plugs.  Bring on those rice boys and their Civic DX's!  (But not the Si...I hate losing.)
Z||000019||Dale||09-01-99||10:36 AM||||A honda cvcc dx, now thats a bad comparison.  It must be time to rebuild your motor.  We just happen to have one of those (the wifes car, 4 doors and seats, one child, what can I say) and it is not very quick.  It might be the plugs HA HA<p>Aloha
Z||000020||theogre||09-01-99||12:31 PM||||PEP boys of all places caries a good wire set. It's a helical core wire with good waranty. I've been using them for years, had only 1 wire fail, and it was from a split boot that let water in. 
Z||000021||Phantom Rage||09-02-99||06:11 AM||||I have tried the Bosch Plat's in my 88 Dis they work nice for the first 2 weeks, afterwards the motor, didn't run as smooth.  I tried the AC delco Rapid Fires, they work GREAT.  I check them whenever I get bored, they are always clean. 
Z||000022||Phantom Rage||09-02-99||06:40 AM||||I have tried the Bosch Plat's in my 88 Dis. They work nice for the first 2 weeks, afterwards the motor, didn't run as smooth.  I checked them and noticed that the plugs for cyl 1&3, or was it 2&4, center electrode worn out.<br>   I tried the AC delco Rapid Fires, they work GREAT.  I check them whenever I get bored, they are always clean. <br>  As far as wires, I use the Jacobs one's, but have had bad experince with them.  Every now and then when I pull off the boots, the inner clip pops off the wire and stays on the plug.  I'm pretty sure it's not the way I pull them off too.  It might be the heat form my header, but I got that ceramic coated.  Luckily Jacobs carries a life time warranty.  I'm not trying to knock Jacob's, they worked great in my Dodge Daytona.  <br>  Look up Magnacore, I believe they make a set for the 4cyl Fiero, not sure about the 6, they probally do.  It's been a long time but I think they are 8.5mm.  I've noticed a lot of forced induction vehichles use these.  The turbo mopar 2.2L and 2.5L crowd seem to like them a lot as well as the import scene.  One draw back is that they cost $60-70.  Not sure about there ohms per foot though.  I'm pretty sure they are low, at least the Jacobs were.  <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
